# Alo King AI Recommendation and Paid Campaign Strategy

## Executive conclusion

Alo King should build two connected but separate systems:

1. A **persistent customer intelligence and recommendation engine** that remembers each customer in a structured, privacy-safe, explainable way.
2. A **restaurant campaign engine** that turns paid broadcasts into measurable, controlled revenue campaigns rather than a simple message-sending feature.

The LLM should be the conversational layer. Deterministic backend services must control truth, money, consent, safety, audience selection, ranking constraints, and delivery.

---

## 1. Persistent customer intelligence

The bot should remember each customer, but memory must be structured, editable, explainable, and privacy-safe.

### Customer signals to store

| Signal | Example |
|---|---|
| Preferred language | Lebanese Arabic, Arabizi, English |
| Preferred villages/addresses | Baakline, Beiteddine, Deir el Qamar |
| Frequent restaurants | A preferred restaurant or cuisine |
| Frequent dishes | Chicken shawarma, family platter |
| Reorder patterns | Usually orders Friday evening |
| Basket patterns | Shawarma often paired with fries and toum |
| Budget range | Usually $10–$20 |
| Dietary preferences | Only when explicitly stated |
| Allergies | Only when explicitly stated; never inferred |
| Household/group context | Family order, office order, individual order |
| Delivery preferences | Call on arrival, leave at gate, preferred landmark |
| Campaign preferences | Global opt-in, muted restaurant, muted sponsored suggestions |
| Feedback | “Too spicy,” “more garlic,” “liked this restaurant” |

### Sensitive-data rule

Never infer that a customer is diabetic, religious, vegetarian, allergic, pregnant, health-conscious, wealthy, or financially constrained. Those attributes may only be stored when explicitly stated, and diet/allergy restrictions must be hard safety constraints rather than marketing signals.

### Four memory layers

#### A. Current conversation memory

What the customer has said in the active conversation, such as:

> “I want something light, no dairy, for three people.”

This controls the immediate recommendation and order flow.

#### B. Durable preference memory

Stable signals learned from explicit statements or repeated behavior:

```text
preferred_language = ar-LB
usual_village = Baakline
favorite_vendor = vendor_123
explicit_preference = “likes spicy food”
```

Every memory should include:

```text
source
confidence
created_at
last_confirmed_at
expires_at
```

#### C. Behavioral history

Orders, reorder frequency, rejected recommendations, completed deliveries, ratings, and campaign responses. Behavioral signals should use recency decay so an old order does not dominate recent behavior.

#### D. Semantic memory

Optional embeddings for free-text feedback such as:

> “Last time the chicken was dry.”

Embeddings are useful for retrieval, but structured Supabase records remain the source of truth.

