## 4. Paid restaurant broadcast strategy

Paid broadcasts should be a campaign product, not a “send messages” button.

```text
Objective
→ eligible audience
→ offer
→ approved creative
→ send-time optimization
→ delivery
→ replies
→ orders
→ attributed revenue
→ campaign report
```

### Campaign objectives

Let the restaurant choose a clear objective:

- reactivate inactive customers;
- promote a new item;
- fill slow periods;
- promote a family bundle;
- increase average order value;
- announce a limited-time offer;
- target customers who ordered a specific item;
- drive orders in a specific village or time period.

The bot should ask what result the restaurant wants, then generate campaign options.

### AI versus backend responsibilities

The AI may propose:

- audience;
- offer wording;
- Arabic and English copy;
- call-to-action;
- send time;
- product;
- campaign title;
- follow-up reply flow.

The backend must control:

- audience eligibility;
- opt-in and opt-out;
- frequency caps;
- pricing;
- menu availability;
- delivery villages;
- campaign budget;
- Meta template category;
- prohibited content;
- merchant balance;
- sending limits;
- refunds;
- attribution.

The model must never decide the final price or charge amount.

### Safest launch audience

At launch, target only:

> Customers who explicitly opted in and previously ordered from that restaurant.

Later segments may include inactive customers, customers who ordered a particular product, frequent Friday customers, customers near the delivery area, and customers who clicked but did not order.

Avoid selling access to vague audiences such as “all people in Baakline.”

