## 5. Excellent restaurant campaign flow

### Step 1 — Objective

Ask:

> “What do you want to achieve?”

### Step 2 — Campaign suggestion

Example:

```text
Objective: Reactivate customers inactive for 30+ days
Audience: 86 eligible opted-in customers
Offer: Family shawarma meal for 4
Recommended send time: Thursday at 5:30 PM
Estimated spend: $X / LBP Y
```

### Step 3 — Preview

Show the exact message in Lebanese Arabic and English, or in the selected customer language where appropriate.

### Step 4 — Validation

Check that:

- the menu item exists;
- the price is current;
- the item is available;
- the restaurant serves the selected villages;
- the offer is not expired;
- the template is approved;
- the content is policy-compliant;
- customer consent is valid;
- frequency caps are respected.

### Step 5 — Merchant confirmation

Require an explicit action:

```text
Confirm and pay
```

Do not send because the owner vaguely says “okay.”

### Step 6 — Controlled delivery

Use pacing, quiet hours, daily and monthly merchant limits, customer frequency caps, automatic pauses when opt-outs or failures spike, and a small test group before broader sending.

### Step 7 — Follow-up

When a customer replies, the Alo King bot should know the campaign and offer context, build the cart, answer questions, hand off when necessary, and track whether the customer ordered.

---

## 6. Measurement: make campaigns excellent through evidence

Do not promise restaurants that every campaign will be perfect. Give them measurable results:

- exact audience size;
- delivery count;
- read count where available;
- reply rate;
- opt-out rate;
- cart-start rate;
- completed-order rate;
- incremental orders;
- revenue;
- campaign cost;
- estimated return on ad spend;
- comparison with a holdout group.

The key metric is:

```text
Incremental completed orders ÷ campaign cost
```

Every meaningful campaign should have a test audience, holdout audience, campaign ID, defined objective, start/end time, and success metric.

